(Bug #66's other edge: with the read inside the function now typed by the
 declaration below it, a call placed ABOVE that declaration reads the global
 mirror while it still holds the zero it starts life with - and a text, list,
 map or buffer read as its own type would dereference that zero. Frame setup
 writes each pointer type's empty value first, which is #25's rule for a name
 whose declaring path may not have run. The first call is uninformative; the
 second, after the declarations, answers properly.)

To 'show all'.
    Print label.
    Print items.
    Print scores.
    Print payload.

'show all'.

a text called label is "hello".
a list called items is [1, 2, 3].
a map called scores is {"a": 1}.
a buffer called payload is "ABC".

'show all'.
